Human–Superintelligence Coexistence: 


A Formal Theory of Autonomy Under Extreme Cognitive Asymmetry

Ouadi Maakoul

AI : ChatGpt, Gemini, Deepseek 

Abstract

This dissertation develops a formal theory of human autonomy under conditions of extreme cognitive asymmetry between biological and artificial intelligence. The central object of analysis is a bounded two-state dynamical system describing the evolution of autonomous human capability A_H(t) and functional dependence on artificial intelligence D_H(t). The theory distinguishes rigorously between autonomous viability—the capacity of a civilization to maintain autonomy without external policy intervention—and controlled viability—the capacity to do so under admissible policy. The unique positive equilibrium of the autonomous system is proven to be globally asymptotically stable via Dulac's criterion combined with the Poincaré–Bendixson theorem and an explicit boundary analysis. The autonomous viability kernel is characterized as the largest invariant subset of the safe set, and its exact geometry is derived for both the positive-margin and zero-margin regimes. The Autonomy Viability Margin \Phi(\rho) is introduced as the scalar quantity governing whether autonomous coexistence remains possible as cognitive asymmetry \rho increases without bound, conditional on an explicit constitutive parameter mapping. The theory distinguishes three layers of autonomy—capability, structural, and constitutional—and is calibrated against empirical findings on AI-induced skill degradation. The central result is that, within the reduced autonomous model, autonomous coexistence is viable if and only if the regeneration rate of autonomous human capability meets or exceeds a critical threshold determined by the atrophy coefficient, dependence dynamics, and the normative autonomy floor.

---

1. The Fundamental Problem

1.1 Intelligence Beyond Biology

Human civilization has historically treated intelligence as a biological property. The emergence of artificial intelligence destabilizes this assumption. Intelligence can increasingly be instantiated, copied, distributed, accelerated, and connected through non-biological substrates.

The critical question is not whether machines will become more intelligent than humans. The question is: what happens to human autonomy when intelligence ceases to be an exclusively biological advantage?

1.2 The Viability Formulation

This dissertation poses the question as a formal viability problem over a bounded dynamical system. The approach is deliberately minimal. We do not attempt to model the full complexity of human–AI civilization. We isolate the smallest dynamical system that captures the essential tension between autonomous capability retention and functional dependence, and we analyze it completely.

The central object is a two-state system (A_H,D_H)\in[0,1]^2:

\dot{A}_H=r_H(1-A_H)-\lambda D_H A_H,
\tag{1.1}

\dot{D}_H=\beta(1-D_H)-\gamma A_H D_H,
\tag{1.2}

where A_H is autonomous capability retention, D_H is functional dependence on AI, and r_H,\lambda,\beta,\gamma are positive parameters whose interpretation and empirical motivation are developed in subsequent chapters.

1.3 The Central Research Question

The dissertation addresses the following question:

\boxed{
\text{Under what conditions can biological and augmented humans remain autonomous}
}

\boxed{
\text{and cooperatively integrated within a civilization containing artificial intelligence}
}

\boxed{
\text{whose cognitive capabilities greatly exceed human capabilities?}
}

A stronger mathematical form is

\boxed{
\exists u(t)\in\mathcal{U}\text{ such that }A_H(t)\ge U_{\min}\ \forall t\ge 0
\text{ along trajectories where }\|\boldsymbol{\rho}(t)\|\to\infty\,?
}

If yes, we seek sufficient conditions and the structure of the viability kernel. If no, we seek the critical boundary \rho_{\mathrm{crit}} at which autonomous coexistence becomes unstable.

1.4 Overview of Results

The principal results of this dissertation are:

1. A corrected global stability theorem for the autonomous two-state system, established via Dulac's criterion, the Poincaré–Bendixson theorem, and an explicit exclusion of boundary limit sets.
2. A complete characterization of the autonomous viability theorem distinguishing r_H<r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, and r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, with a rigorous if-and-only-if characterization of autonomous viability.
3. An exact geometric characterization of the autonomous viability kernel \mathcal{V}_0 as the largest invariant subset of the safe set, including its boundary structure in both the positive-margin and zero-margin regimes.
4. A rigorous separation of the autonomous viability kernel \mathcal{V}_0 from the controlled viability kernel \mathcal{V}_u, with a control-barrier-function formulation.
5. The Autonomy Viability Margin \Phi(\rho) as the central scalar quantity governing coexistence under increasing cognitive asymmetry, conditional on an explicit constitutive parameter mapping.
6. A three-layer architecture of autonomy—capability, structural, constitutional—that separates state autonomy, exit and institutional capacity, and legitimate authority.
7. Empirical calibration against published findings on colonoscopy deskilling, aviation manual flying, and cognitive offloading.

