4. Global Stability of the Autonomous System

4.1 Equilibrium Analysis

At equilibrium, \dot{A}_H=0 and \dot{D}_H=0. Therefore:

r_H(1-A_H^*)=\lambda D_H^*A_H^*,
\tag{4.1}

\beta(1-D_H^*)=\gamma A_H^*D_H^*.
\tag{4.2}

From (4.1):

\boxed{A_H^*=\frac{r_H}{r_H+\lambda D_H^*}.}
\tag{4.3}

From (4.2):

\boxed{D_H^*=\frac{\beta}{\beta+\gamma A_H^*}.}
\tag{4.4}

4.2 Uniqueness

Substituting (4.4) into (4.3):

A_H^*=\frac{r_H}{r_H+\lambda\frac{\beta}{\beta+\gamma A_H^*}}.

Multiplying through:

A_H^*\left[r_H+\frac{\lambda\beta}{\beta+\gamma A_H^*}\right]=r_H.

Multiplying by \beta+\gamma A_H^*:

r_H A_H^*(\beta+\gamma A_H^*)+\lambda\beta A_H^*=r_H(\beta+\gamma A_H^*).

Expanding:

r_H\beta A_H^*+r_H\gamma(A_H^*)^2+\lambda\beta A_H^*=r_H\beta+r_H\gamma A_H^*.

Rearranging:

r_H\gamma(A_H^*)^2+[\beta(r_H+\lambda)-r_H\gamma]A_H^*-r_H\beta=0.
\tag{4.5}

This is a quadratic in A_H^* with negative constant term -r_H\beta<0. Therefore the two roots have opposite signs. There is exactly one positive equilibrium:

\boxed{
A_H^*=\frac{-[\beta(r_H+\lambda)-r_H\gamma]+\sqrt{[\beta(r_H+\lambda)-r_H\gamma]^2+4r_H^2\beta\gamma}}{2r_H\gamma}
}
\tag{4.6}

and

\boxed{D_H^*=\frac{\beta}{\beta+\gamma A_H^*}.}
\tag{4.7}

Proposition 4.1 (Uniqueness). The system (1.1)–(1.2) has exactly one equilibrium in (0,1)^2.

Proof. The quadratic (4.5) has exactly one positive root. The corresponding D_H^* given by (4.7) lies in (0,1) because \beta>0 and \gamma A_H^*>0. \square

4.3 Local Stability

The Jacobian at equilibrium is

J(A_H^*,D_H^*)=
\begin{pmatrix}
-r_H-\lambda D_H^* & -\lambda A_H^*\\
-\gamma D_H^* & -\beta-\gamma A_H^*
\end{pmatrix}.

The trace is

\operatorname{tr}(J)=-r_H-\lambda D_H^*-\beta-\gamma A_H^*<0.

The determinant is

\det(J)=(r_H+\lambda D_H^*)(\beta+\gamma A_H^*)-\lambda\gamma A_H^*D_H^*.

Expanding:

\det(J)=r_H\beta+r_H\gamma A_H^*+\lambda\beta D_H^*>0.

Therefore

\boxed{\operatorname{tr}(J)<0,\qquad\det(J)>0.}

The equilibrium is locally asymptotically stable.

4.4 Boundary Analysis

The system is not cooperative: the off-diagonal entries of the Jacobian are negative, so it is competitive. Global asymptotic stability requires an argument beyond linearization.

We first establish the invariance and boundary structure of the domain [0,1]^2.

On the edge A_H=0:

\dot{A}_H\big|_{A_H=0}=r_H>0,

so trajectories leave this edge into the interior.

On the edge A_H=1:

\dot{A}_H\big|_{A_H=1}=-\lambda D_H<0\quad\text{for }D_H>0,

so trajectories enter the interior from this edge. At the corner (A_H,D_H)=(1,0):

\dot{A}_H=0,\qquad \dot{D}_H=\beta>0,

so the corner is not invariant.

On the edge D_H=0:

\dot{D}_H\big|_{D_H=0}=\beta>0,

so trajectories leave this edge into the interior.

On the edge D_H=1:

\dot{D}_H\big|_{D_H=1}=-\gamma A_H<0\quad\text{for }A_H>0,

so trajectories enter the interior from this edge. At the corner (A_H,D_H)=(0,1), \dot{A}_H=r_H>0, so the corner is not invariant.

Lemma 4.2 (No invariant boundary components). The boundary \partial([0,1]^2) contains no invariant subset supporting a nontrivial limiting trajectory. The only invariant subsets of \partial([0,1]^2) are the corners, none of which is invariant under the full flow.

Proof. As computed above, on each edge the vector field points strictly into the interior except at isolated corners, and at each corner at least one component of the vector field points into the interior. Hence no edge or corner is invariant. \square

4.5 Global Asymptotic Stability

We now establish global convergence. The argument proceeds in three steps: exclusion of periodic orbits via Dulac's criterion, exclusion of boundary limit sets via Lemma 4.2, and application of the Poincaré–Bendixson theorem.

Theorem 4.3 (Global Asymptotic Stability). The unique equilibrium (A_H^*,D_H^*) is globally asymptotically stable in (0,1)^2.

Proof. The domain (0,1)^2 is simply connected and forward-invariant (by the boundary analysis of §4.4). Define the Dulac multiplier

B(A_H,D_H)=\frac{1}{A_H D_H}.

Then

B\dot{A}_H=\frac{r_H(1-A_H)}{A_H D_H}-\lambda,

B\dot{D}_H=\frac{\beta(1-D_H)}{A_H D_H}-\gamma.

Compute the divergence:

\frac{\partial}{\partial A_H}\left(\frac{r_H(1-A_H)}{A_H D_H}-\lambda\right)
=
r_H\frac{\partial}{\partial A_H}\left(\frac{1-A_H}{A_H D_H}\right)
=
-\frac{r_H}{A_H^2 D_H}.

Similarly,

\frac{\partial}{\partial D_H}\left(\frac{\beta(1-D_H)}{A_H D_H}-\gamma\right)
=
-\frac{\beta}{A_H D_H^2}.

Therefore

\nabla\cdot(BF)=-\frac{r_H}{A_H^2 D_H}-\frac{\beta}{A_H D_H^2}<0

for all (A_H,D_H)\in(0,1)^2. By Dulac's criterion, the system has no periodic orbits in (0,1)^2, and no homoclinic or heteroclinic cycles in the interior.

It remains to exclude boundary limit sets. By Lemma 4.2, no invariant subset of \partial([0,1]^2) supports a nontrivial trajectory. Since the vector field points strictly into the interior on all edges (except at isolated non-invariant corners), any trajectory that reaches the boundary immediately enters the interior and remains there.

We now apply the Poincaré–Bendixson theorem. Let x_0\in(0,1)^2. The forward trajectory \phi_t(x_0) is contained in the compact set [0,1]^2. Its \omega-limit set \omega(x_0) is nonempty, compact, and invariant. Since the system is planar and has no periodic orbits in (0,1)^2, and since the boundary supports no invariant limit sets, \omega(x_0) must be the set of equilibria in (0,1)^2. By Proposition 4.1, this set is the singleton \{(A_H^*,D_H^*)\}. Therefore every trajectory converges to the unique equilibrium.

Combined with local asymptotic stability (§4.3), this establishes global asymptotic stability. \square

Remark 4.4. The Dulac argument alone excludes periodic orbits but does not by itself establish convergence. The Poincaré–Bendixson theorem, combined with the boundary analysis of Lemma 4.2, supplies the missing step.

Corollary 4.5. The basin of attraction of (A_H^*,D_H^*) is all of (0,1)^2.

