5. The Autonomous Viability Theorem

5.1 The Safe Set

Let U_{\min}\in(0,1) be the normative autonomy floor. Define the safe set

\mathcal{K}=\{(A_H,D_H)\in[0,1]^2:A_H\ge U_{\min}\}.

Human autonomy is viable if

\boxed{A_H(t)\ge U_{\min}\quad\forall t\ge 0.}
\tag{5.1}

5.2 The Autonomous Viability Kernel

Definition 5.1. The autonomous viability kernel \mathcal{V}_0 is the set of initial states (A_H(0),D_H(0))\in\mathcal{K} from which the autonomous trajectory of (1.1)–(1.2) remains in \mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0:

\mathcal{V}_0=\{(A_H(0),D_H(0))\in\mathcal{K}:A_H(t)\ge U_{\min}\ \forall t\ge 0\}.

Equivalently, \mathcal{V}_0 is the largest invariant subset of \mathcal{K} under the autonomous dynamics.

Remark 5.2. The autonomous viability kernel is not the basin of attraction of the viable equilibrium. The basin of attraction may include states with A_H<U_{\min}, which are not in \mathcal{V}_0 because they violate the viability constraint immediately. The correct relationship is

\mathcal{V}_0\subseteq\mathcal{B}(A_H^*,D_H^*)\cap\mathcal{K},

where \mathcal{B}(A_H^*,D_H^*)=(0,1)^2 is the basin of attraction. The inclusion is strict in general.

5.3 Boundary Analysis

At the boundary A_H=U_{\min}, the A_H-velocity is

\dot{A}_H=r_H(1-U_{\min})-\lambda D_H U_{\min}.

The dependence level at which the vector field is tangent to the autonomy boundary is

\boxed{D_{\mathrm{crit}}=\frac{r_H(1-U_{\min})}{\lambda U_{\min}}.}
\tag{5.2}

At the point (A_H,D_H)=(U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}}), the vector field is tangent to the boundary A_H=U_{\min}. For the trajectory to remain in \mathcal{K} at this point, we require \dot{D}_H\le 0:

\beta(1-D_{\mathrm{crit}})-\gamma U_{\min}D_{\mathrm{crit}}\le 0.

Rearranging:

D_{\mathrm{crit}}\ge\frac{\beta}{\beta+\gamma U_{\min}}.
\tag{5.3}

Substituting (5.2):

\frac{r_H(1-U_{\min})}{\lambda U_{\min}}\ge\frac{\beta}{\beta+\gamma U_{\min}}.

Therefore:

\boxed{r_H(1-U_{\min})(\beta+\gamma U_{\min})\ge\lambda\beta U_{\min}.}
\tag{5.4}

This is the central inequality of the autonomous system.

5.4 Critical Regeneration Rate

Solving (5.4) for r_H:

\boxed{r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}=\frac{\lambda\beta U_{\min}}{(1-U_{\min})(\beta+\gamma U_{\min})}.}
\tag{5.5}

5.5 The Corrected Theorem

Theorem 5.3 (Autonomous Viability). Let the autonomous system (1.1)–(1.2) be defined on [0,1]^2 with r_H,\lambda,\beta,\gamma>0 and U_{\min}\in(0,1). Then:

(A) If r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, the equilibrium satisfies A_H^*>U_{\min}, and \mathcal{V}_0\neq\varnothing with nonempty interior.

(B) If r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, the equilibrium satisfies A_H^*=U_{\min}, and \mathcal{V}_0\neq\varnothing with nonempty interior in \mathcal{K}. The equilibrium lies on the boundary of the safe set.

(C) If r_H<r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, the equilibrium satisfies A_H^*<U_{\min}, and \mathcal{V}_0=\varnothing.

Proof.

(A) The viability condition (5.4) holds strictly when r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}. At equilibrium, A_H^* satisfies (4.3). The condition A_H^*>U_{\min} is equivalent to (5.4). Since the equilibrium is globally asymptotically stable (Theorem 4.3) and lies strictly inside \mathcal{K}, there exists an open neighborhood of the equilibrium contained in \mathcal{K} whose trajectories converge to the equilibrium without leaving \mathcal{K}. Therefore \mathcal{V}_0 contains an open neighborhood of the equilibrium and hence has nonempty interior.

(B) When r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, inequality (5.4) holds with equality. The equilibrium satisfies A_H^*=U_{\min} and D_H^*=D_{\mathrm{crit}}. The equilibrium itself is in \mathcal{K}, so \mathcal{V}_0\neq\varnothing. The equilibrium lies on the boundary \partial\mathcal{K}. Since the equilibrium is globally asymptotically stable, every trajectory in (0,1)^2 converges to (U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}}). The kernel \mathcal{V}_0 consists of those initial conditions whose trajectories converge to the equilibrium without crossing A_H=U_{\min}. The precise characterization of \mathcal{V}_0 in this case is given in §6.

(C) When r_H<r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, inequality (5.4) is violated. The equilibrium satisfies A_H^*<U_{\min}. By Theorem 4.3, every trajectory converges to this equilibrium. Since the equilibrium lies strictly below the safe set, every trajectory crosses A_H=U_{\min} in finite time. Therefore no trajectory remains in \mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0, and \mathcal{V}_0=\varnothing. \square

Corollary 5.4. \mathcal{V}_0\neq\varnothing\iff r_H\ge r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}.

5.6 Critical Atrophy Coefficient and Dependence Threshold

Solving (5.4) for \lambda:

\boxed{\lambda_{\mathrm{crit}}=\frac{r_H(1-U_{\min})(\beta+\gamma U_{\min})}{\beta U_{\min}}.}
\tag{5.6}

Then

\lambda\le\lambda_{\mathrm{crit}}\iff A_H^*\ge U_{\min}.

Solving (5.2) for D_{\mathrm{crit}}:

\boxed{D_{\mathrm{crit}}=\frac{r_H(1-U_{\min})}{\lambda U_{\min}}.}
\tag{5.7}

The civilization remains locally viable at the autonomy boundary if D_H<D_{\mathrm{crit}}. It loses viability through the boundary if D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}}.

5.7 Comparative Statics

From (5.7):

· More regeneration: r_H\uparrow\Rightarrow D_{\mathrm{crit}}\uparrow. Humanity can tolerate more dependence.
· Stronger atrophy: \lambda\uparrow\Rightarrow D_{\mathrm{crit}}\downarrow. Less dependence becomes tolerable.
· Higher autonomy requirement: U_{\min}\uparrow\Rightarrow D_{\mathrm{crit}}\downarrow. The stricter the definition of autonomy, the less dependence can be tolerated.
· Faster dependence accumulation: \beta\uparrow\Rightarrow r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}\uparrow. The derivative is

\frac{\partial r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}}{\partial\beta}
=\frac{\lambda\gamma U_{\min}^2}{(1-U_{\min})(\beta+\gamma U_{\min})^2}>0.

· Stronger dependence reduction: \gamma\uparrow\Rightarrow r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}\downarrow.

