6. Exact Autonomous Viability Kernel

6.1 The Critical Trajectory and Separatrix

The autonomous viability kernel \mathcal{V}_0 is the largest invariant subset of the safe set \mathcal{K}=\{(A_H,D_H):A_H\ge U_{\min}\} under the autonomous dynamics (1.1)–(1.2).

Definition 6.1 (Critical trajectory). Let \Gamma:\mathbb{R}\to[0,1]^2 be the maximal trajectory of (1.1)–(1.2) satisfying

\Gamma(0)=(U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}}).

Denote by \Gamma_+=\{\Gamma(t):t\ge 0\} the forward branch and by \Gamma_-=\{\Gamma(t):t<0\} the backward branch.

Lemma 6.2 (Forward branch). The forward branch \Gamma_+ is contained in \mathcal{K} and converges to the equilibrium (A_H^*,D_H^*).

Proof. At t=0, \dot{A}_H=0 and \dot{D}_H\le 0. The second-order derivative of A_H along \Gamma at t=0 is

\ddot{A}_H\big|_{\Gamma(0)}
=
-(r_H+\lambda D_H)\dot{A}_H-\lambda A_H\dot{D}_H
=
-\lambda U_{\min}\dot{D}_H.

When r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, \dot{D}_H<0, so \ddot{A}_H>0. Hence the trajectory enters the interior \{A_H>U_{\min}\} immediately forward of t=0. By Theorem 4.3, the trajectory converges to (A_H^*,D_H^*). Since A_H^*>U_{\min} when r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}} and A_H^*=U_{\min} when r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, the forward branch remains in \mathcal{K}. \square

Lemma 6.3 (Backward branch). The backward branch \Gamma_- enters the region \{A_H>U_{\min}\}\cap\{D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}}\} and extends to \partial([0,1]^2).

Proof. In backward time, the vector field is (-\dot{A}_H,-\dot{D}_H). At \Gamma(0), -\dot{A}_H=0 and -\dot{D}_H\ge 0. For t<0 small, D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}} and A_H=U_{\min}. In this region, \dot{A}_H<0, so -\dot{A}_H>0, and the backward trajectory moves to A_H>U_{\min}. The backward trajectory is bounded (since [0,1]^2 is compact). Its \alpha-limit set (in backward time) is contained in \partial([0,1]^2). By Lemma 4.2, the boundary contains no invariant limit sets, so the backward trajectory must approach \partial([0,1]^2). \square

6.2 Exact Characterization

Theorem 6.4 (Exact autonomous viability kernel). Let r_H\ge r_H^{\mathrm{crit}} and U_{\min}\in(0,1). Then:

\mathcal{V}_0
=
\{(A_H,D_H)\in\mathcal{K}:
\phi_t(A_H,D_H)\in\mathcal{K}\ \forall t\ge 0\}

and \mathcal{V}_0 has the following structure:

(i) \mathcal{V}_0 is closed in [0,1]^2, forward-invariant, and contains the equilibrium (A_H^*,D_H^*).

(ii) The segment

\Sigma_0=\{(U_{\min},D_H):0\le D_H\le D_{\mathrm{crit}}\}

is contained in \partial\mathcal{V}_0\cap\partial\mathcal{K}.

(iii) The backward branch \Gamma_- of the critical trajectory is contained in \partial\mathcal{V}_0\cap\mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K}) and forms the upper boundary of \mathcal{V}_0 within the interior.

(iv) When r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, \mathcal{V}_0 has nonempty interior in \mathcal{K}, contains an open neighborhood of (A_H^*,D_H^*), and

\mathcal{V}_0
=
\mathcal{K}\setminus\mathcal{E},

where \mathcal{E} is the open set of initial conditions whose trajectories cross \{A_H=U_{\min},D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}}\}.

(v) When r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, \mathcal{V}_0 has nonempty interior in \mathcal{K}. The equilibrium E^*=(U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}}) lies on \partial\mathcal{K}. The boundary of \mathcal{V}_0 within \mathcal{K} consists of the segment \Sigma_0 on \partial\mathcal{K} together with a separatrix curve \Gamma in \mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K}) that approaches E^* tangentially and forms the upper boundary of \mathcal{V}_0.

Proof.

(i) Closedness follows from the definition: \mathcal{V}_0 is the intersection of \mathcal{K} with the set of initial conditions whose trajectories remain in \mathcal{K}, which is closed by continuity of the flow. Forward invariance is immediate from the definition. The equilibrium (A_H^*,D_H^*) is in \mathcal{K} (since A_H^*\ge U_{\min}) and is a fixed point, so it is in \mathcal{V}_0.

(ii) For (U_{\min},D_H) with D_H<D_{\mathrm{crit}}, \dot{A}_H>0, so the trajectory enters \{A_H>U_{\min}\}. Since the equilibrium has A_H^*\ge U_{\min}, the trajectory converges without crossing A_H=U_{\min} again. Hence \Sigma_0\subseteq\mathcal{V}_0. For D_H slightly greater than D_{\mathrm{crit}}, \dot{A}_H<0, so the trajectory exits \mathcal{K} immediately; hence \Sigma_0\subseteq\partial\mathcal{V}_0.

(iii) Let x\in\Gamma_-. Its forward trajectory passes through \Gamma(0)=(U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}}) and then remains in \mathcal{K} by Lemma 6.2. Hence x\in\mathcal{V}_0. Points in a neighborhood of x on the "upper" side (larger D_H for fixed A_H) have trajectories that cross A_H=U_{\min} at D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}} and exit \mathcal{K}. Hence x\in\partial\mathcal{V}_0.

(iv) When r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, A_H^*>U_{\min}, and the equilibrium is strictly interior to \mathcal{K}. By Theorem 4.3, the basin of attraction is all of (0,1)^2. By continuity, there exists \epsilon>0 such that the ball B_\epsilon(A_H^*,D_H^*)\subseteq\mathcal{K} and every trajectory starting in this ball remains in \mathcal{K}. Hence \mathcal{V}_0 has nonempty interior. The complement \mathcal{E}=\mathcal{K}\setminus\mathcal{V}_0 consists of initial conditions whose trajectories exit \mathcal{K}, which occurs precisely by crossing \{A_H=U_{\min},D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}}\}.

(v) When r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, A_H^*=U_{\min} and D_H^*=D_{\mathrm{crit}}. The equilibrium lies on \partial\mathcal{K}. Consider the point (U_{\min},D_H^*-\epsilon) for small \epsilon>0. At this point,

\dot{A}_H=r_H(1-U_{\min})-\lambda(D_H^*-\epsilon)U_{\min}
=\lambda U_{\min}\epsilon>0,

so A_H increases and the trajectory enters \{A_H>U_{\min}\}. Since E^* is globally asymptotically stable (Theorem 4.3), the trajectory converges to E^*. Because it entered the interior of \mathcal{K} and E^* lies on \partial\mathcal{K}, the trajectory must approach E^* from the region \{A_H>U_{\min}\}. Therefore it remains in \mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0, so (U_{\min},D_H^*-\epsilon)\in\mathcal{V}_0. This establishes that \mathcal{V}_0 contains a neighborhood of the segment \Sigma_0 in \mathcal{K}, and hence has nonempty interior.

The boundary of \mathcal{V}_0 within \mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K}) is a separatrix curve \Gamma that approaches E^* tangentially. Points above \Gamma (larger D_H for fixed A_H) have trajectories that cross \{A_H=U_{\min},D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}}\} and exit \mathcal{K}. Points below \Gamma have trajectories that converge to E^* without crossing the exit segment. \square

Remark 6.5. The critical case r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}} represents a zero-margin viability state: the equilibrium is viable but lies on \partial\mathcal{K}. Any perturbation that pushes the state into \{A_H<U_{\min}\} leads to exit. The viability kernel has nonempty interior but the equilibrium is not robustly viable.

Remark 6.6. The statement in earlier versions that "the viability kernel is one-dimensional in the critical case" was incorrect. The stable manifold of the equilibrium is two-dimensional (the entire basin), and the viability kernel is a proper subset of the basin intersected with \mathcal{K}. The correct geometric description is given by Theorem 6.4(v).

6.3 Summary of Regimes

Regime Condition \mathcal{V}_0 Interpretation
Positive margin r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}} Nonempty interior, equilibrium strictly interior Robust autonomous viability
Zero margin r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}} Nonempty interior, equilibrium on \partial\mathcal{K} Fragile viability
No viability r_H<r_H^{\mathrm{crit}} Empty Autonomous collapse

