7. Controlled Viability

7.1 The Control Problem

The autonomous analysis assumes fixed parameters. In reality, policy can influence r_H,\lambda,\beta,\gamma. Let u(t)\in\mathcal{U}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^m be a control input, and let

r_H=r_H(u),\quad\lambda=\lambda(u),\quad\beta=\beta(u),\quad\gamma=\gamma(u).

The controlled dynamics are

\dot{A}_H=r_H(u)(1-A_H)-\lambda(u)D_H A_H,
\tag{7.1}

\dot{D}_H=\beta(u)(1-D_H)-\gamma(u)A_H D_H.
\tag{7.2}

7.2 Controlled Viability Kernel

Definition 7.1. The controlled viability kernel \mathcal{V}_u is

\mathcal{V}_u=\{(A_H(0),D_H(0))\in\mathcal{K}:\exists u(\cdot)\in\mathcal{U}\text{ such that }A_H(t)\ge U_{\min}\ \forall t\ge 0\}.

7.3 Relation to Autonomous Viability

Proposition 7.2. \mathcal{V}_0\subseteq\mathcal{V}_u.

Proof. Any trajectory that remains in \mathcal{K} under the autonomous dynamics is also admissible under the controlled dynamics (take u(t) constant). \square

Remark 7.3. The inclusion is strict in general. If r_H<r_H^{\mathrm{crit}} for the autonomous system, a control u(t) may still maintain viability by increasing r_H(u), decreasing \lambda(u), or otherwise altering the parameters. Therefore r_H<r_H^{\mathrm{crit}} does not imply \mathcal{V}_u=\varnothing. The precise characterization of when the inclusion is strict is an open problem.

7.4 Control Barrier Function Formulation

Let h(A_H,D_H)=A_H-U_{\min}. The safe set is \mathcal{C}=\{h\ge 0\}. The function h is a control barrier function if there exists an extended class-\mathcal{K} function \alpha such that

\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\left[\dot{A}_H+\alpha(h)\right]\ge 0

for all (A_H,D_H)\in\mathcal{C}. Equivalently:

\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\left[r_H(u)(1-A_H)-\lambda(u)D_H A_H+\alpha(A_H-U_{\min})\right]\ge 0.

The controlled viability kernel \mathcal{V}_u is the zero superlevel set of the maximal control barrier function.

7.5 Backward Reachability

For numerical computation, \mathcal{V}_u can be computed via backward reachability. Define the value function

V(t,x)=\inf_{u(\cdot)\in\mathcal{U}}\left[\min_{s\in[0,t]}(A_H(s)-U_{\min})\right].

The controlled viability kernel is

\mathcal{V}_u=\{x:V(\infty,x)\ge 0\}.

Standard level-set methods can approximate \mathcal{V}_u on a discretized state space.

---

8. The Autonomy Viability Margin and Asymmetry Scaling

8.1 Definition

Define the Autonomy Viability Margin:

\boxed{\Phi(\rho)=r_H(\rho)(1-U_{\min})[\beta(\rho)+\gamma(\rho)U_{\min}]-\lambda(\rho)\beta(\rho)U_{\min}.}
\tag{8.1}

Then:

· \Phi(\rho)>0: positive autonomy margin. The equilibrium lies strictly above U_{\min}, and \mathcal{V}_0 has nonempty interior.
· \Phi(\rho)=0: critical boundary. The equilibrium lies exactly at U_{\min}, and \mathcal{V}_0 has nonempty interior but the equilibrium is on \partial\mathcal{K}.
· \Phi(\rho)<0: the stable equilibrium lies below the autonomy floor, and \mathcal{V}_0=\varnothing.

8.2 The Constitutive Mapping

The Autonomy Viability Margin \Phi depends on \rho only through the four parameter functions r_H(\rho),\lambda(\rho),\beta(\rho),\gamma(\rho). These functions are not derived from the model. They constitute an external constitutive mapping

\rho\longmapsto\{r_H(\rho),\lambda(\rho),\beta(\rho),\gamma(\rho)\}

that must be specified empirically or assumed exogenously. The mathematical results of this chapter are conditional on this mapping.

Remark 8.1. The scalar asymmetry summary \rho is a normative construct (it depends on weights w_i). The constitutive mapping from \rho to the dynamical parameters is a separate modeling choice. Neither is derived from the autonomous two-state system.

8.3 The Central Asymmetry Question

The central research question can now be posed as:

\boxed{\liminf_{\rho\to\infty}\Phi(\rho)>0\,?}
\tag{8.2}

· If yes: persistent viability. Extreme cognitive asymmetry does not eliminate autonomous human states.
· If \Phi(\rho_{\mathrm{crit}})=0 for some finite \rho_{\mathrm{crit}}: critical asymmetry. There exists a threshold beyond which autonomy collapses.
· If \Phi(\rho)\to-\infty: inevitable loss of viability under the assumed parameter scaling.

This replaces the vague question "Can humans coexist with superintelligence?" with a precise mathematical condition conditional on a specified constitutive mapping.

8.4 Scaling Analysis

Proposition 8.2. Suppose r_H(\rho)\le M<\infty for all \rho, while \lambda(\rho)\to\infty. If \beta(\rho) and \gamma(\rho) remain bounded below by positive constants, then \Phi(\rho)<0 for sufficiently large \rho.

Proof. Under the stated conditions, the negative term -\lambda(\rho)\beta(\rho)U_{\min} diverges to -\infty, while the positive term remains bounded. Therefore \Phi(\rho)<0 for sufficiently large \rho. \square

Remark 8.3. If \beta(\rho)\to 0 sufficiently fast, the divergence of \lambda(\rho) may be offset. The scaling assumptions must be stated jointly for all four parameters.

Proposition 8.4. If

r_H(\rho)>\frac{\lambda(\rho)\beta(\rho)U_{\min}}{(1-U_{\min})(\beta(\rho)+\gamma(\rho)U_{\min})}

for all \rho, then \mathcal{V}_0(\rho)\neq\varnothing for all \rho.

Proof. The condition is exactly r_H(\rho)>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}(\rho). By Theorem 5.3(A), \mathcal{V}_0(\rho)\neq\varnothing. \square

8.5 Bifurcation Structure

The equation \Phi(\rho)=0 defines the critical asymmetry threshold. When the constitutive mapping is specified, the bifurcation structure of \Phi can be analyzed. In the simplest case where r_H is constant and \lambda(\rho) increases linearly while \beta,\gamma remain constant, there is a unique critical value

\rho_{\mathrm{crit}}=\frac{r_H(1-U_{\min})(\beta+\gamma U_{\min})-\lambda_0\beta U_{\min}}{k_\lambda\beta U_{\min}},

where \lambda(\rho)=\lambda_0+k_\lambda\rho. Beyond this value, autonomous viability fails.

