9. Three Layers of Autonomy

9.1 Motivation

The reduced model captures state autonomy—the capacity of humans to perform critical functions without AI. But autonomy has additional dimensions that the two-state system does not capture. This chapter elevates these dimensions into the formal architecture.

9.2 Capability Autonomy

A_H(t)\in[0,1]

Capability autonomy is the capacity of humans to perform critical cognitive and physical functions without AI. It is the variable governed by the atrophy dynamics (1.1)–(1.2). Its viability condition is A_H\ge U_{\min}.

9.3 Structural Autonomy

(X_H,R,M_H)\in[0,1]^3

Structural autonomy is the capacity of humans to exit AI-mediated systems (X_H), to maintain independent institutions (R), and to reconstruct critical knowledge independently (M_H). It determines whether humans can exercise the exit option and rebuild capability after a discontinuity in the AI substrate.

Structural autonomy enters the reduced model through the parameters:

· M_H affects r_H: high institutional memory enables regeneration.
· X_H affects \gamma: high exit capacity enables effective reduction of dependence.
· R affects the enforceability of constitutional constraints.

9.4 Constitutional Autonomy

U_H\in[0,1]

Constitutional autonomy is the legitimate authority of humans over decisions affecting their collective future. It is not reducible to capability or structural autonomy. A civilization may retain high capability autonomy and high structural autonomy while losing constitutional autonomy—if decisions are effectively delegated to AI systems whose outputs humans cannot meaningfully contest.

Constitutional autonomy is a normative-political variable. Its formalization requires a theory of legitimacy that is beyond the scope of this dissertation. The dissertation treats U_{\min} as a normative floor on capability autonomy and identifies constitutional autonomy as an open problem.

9.5 Layer Architecture

The three layers form a hierarchy:

\boxed{
\text{Capability Autonomy}
\rightarrow
\text{Structural Autonomy}
\rightarrow
\text{Constitutional Autonomy}
}

Failure can occur at any layer:

· Capability failure: A_H<U_{\min}. The reduced model's central concern.
· Structural failure: X_H=0 or M_H=0 or R=0. Humans cannot exit, rebuild, or constrain.
· Constitutional failure: U_H collapses even if A_H and structural variables remain high.

Proposition 9.1 (Necessary conditions for joint autonomy). Within the reduced model and its structural extensions:

\text{Capability Autonomy viable}\iff r_H\ge r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}.

\text{Structural Autonomy viable}\iff X_H>0,\ R>0,\ M_H>0.

\text{Constitutional Autonomy viable}\iff U_H\ge U_{\min}^{(c)}

for some normative constitutional floor U_{\min}^{(c)}.

9.6 Corrected List of Structural Conditions

The four conditions identified in earlier versions—

r_H\ge r_H^{\mathrm{crit}},\quad X_H>0,\quad R>0,\quad M_H>0

—operate at different conceptual levels. A rigorous formulation separates:

1. Capability condition (reduced model): r_H\ge r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}.
2. Structural conditions (institutional capacity): X_H>0, R>0, M_H>0.
3. Constitutional condition (legitimacy): U_H\ge U_{\min}^{(c)}.

The theory establishes formal results only for the first. The second and third require additional modeling and normative analysis.

