10. Empirical Calibration

10.1 The Calibration Problem

The parameters r_H,\lambda,\beta,\gamma are not directly observable. They must be estimated from empirical proxies. The current calibration is qualitative: it establishes the direction of effects and the plausibility of parameter ranges, but not point estimates.

10.2 Colonoscopy Deskilling

In a multicenter randomized trial, the adenoma detection rate (ADR) dropped from 28.4% to 22.4% when endoscopists reverted to non-AI procedures after repeated AI use—a 21% relative decline. This provides an empirical proxy for the atrophy coefficient \lambda:

\lambda\approx\frac{\Delta\text{ADR}}{\text{ADR}\cdot D_H\cdot T},

where T is the duration of AI use and D_H is the level of dependence. Conversion to model units requires assumptions about time scale and dependence level.

10.3 Aviation Manual Flying

FOQA/FDM indicators show manual flight represents on average less than five minutes of total flight time, with measurable degradation of manual flying skills. Proficiency returns quickly with practice—up to 75% in as little as five minutes. This provides empirical proxies for both \lambda and r_H:

r_H\approx\frac{1}{\tau_{\text{recovery}}},

where \tau_{\text{recovery}} is the characteristic recovery time.

10.4 Cognitive Offloading

Empirical studies on cognitive offloading and human–AI interaction provide evidence for the feedback loop between behavioral shifts and cognitive dependency, validating the coupled structure of equations (1.1) and (1.2). The key qualitative finding is that increased reliance on AI reduces independent performance, which in turn increases reliance—the positive feedback loop captured by the \lambda D_H A_H term.

10.5 Calibration Table

Parameter Empirical Proxy Mapping Assumption Uncertainty
r_H Aviation proficiency recovery rate Recovery time maps to 1/r_H Moderate
\lambda Colonoscopy ADR decline Decline rate maps to \lambda D_H High
\beta AI adoption rate Adoption curve maps to \beta Moderate
\gamma Exit capacity effect Institutional data required High

Remark 10.1. The mapping assumptions are non-trivial and constitute an open research problem. The current calibration is qualitative; a quantitative calibration requires longitudinal data and explicit mapping assumptions.

