11. Open Problems

11.1 Vector Viability Kernel

Compute the viability kernel for the full vector-valued asymmetry \boldsymbol{\rho}, not just the scalar summary \rho. This requires extending the two-state system to a higher-dimensional system with vector-valued asymmetry.

11.2 Stochastic Dynamics

Add environmental shocks \epsilon(t) and compute the stochastic viability kernel. The stochastic viability kernel is the set of initial states from which there exists a policy such that the probability of remaining in the safe set exceeds a specified threshold.

11.3 Exact Controlled Viability Kernel

Characterize precisely when the inclusion \mathcal{V}_0\subseteq\mathcal{V}_u is strict. This requires solving the control barrier function problem explicitly for the two-state system.

11.4 Game-Theoretic Integration

Embed the dynamical system in a repeated game with incomplete information and compute the equilibrium governance architectures. This requires explicit strategy spaces, information sets, and punishment technologies.

11.5 Empirical Estimation of Parameters

Estimate r_H,\lambda,\beta,\gamma from longitudinal data on AI adoption, cognitive offloading, and institutional resilience. The current calibration is qualitative; a quantitative calibration requires time-series data and explicit mapping assumptions.

11.6 Constitutive Mapping Identification

Identify the constitutive mapping \rho\mapsto(r_H,\lambda,\beta,\gamma) empirically. This is the central open problem connecting the mathematical model to real-world predictions.

11.7 Normative Weights

Justify the weights w_i in the scalar asymmetry summary through deliberative or constitutional procedures. The weights are normative and cannot be derived from the mathematical model alone.

11.8 Constitutional Autonomy

Develop a formal theory of constitutional autonomy U_H that is independent of capability autonomy A_H and structural autonomy (X_H,R,M_H). This requires a theory of legitimacy and contestability.

11.9 Global Stability Under Control

Prove global asymptotic stability for the controlled system under admissible control laws. The current global stability result applies to the autonomous system; the controlled system requires a separate analysis.

11.10 The Boundary Case r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}

Analyze the behavior of the system at the critical boundary in the presence of noise and finite-time perturbations. The equilibrium lies on the boundary of the safe set, and the viability kernel has nonempty interior; perturbations may push the system into the non-viable region.

---

12. Conclusion

12.1 Summary of Results

The problem is not necessarily

\text{Humans vs. Superintelligence}.

A more useful representation is

\boxed{\text{Humans}+\text{Augmentation}+\text{Collective Intelligence}+\text{Institutions}+\text{Memory}+\text{Artificial Intelligence}.}

The future of biological humanity may depend less on achieving equality with artificial intelligence than on discovering whether autonomy, cooperation, and pluralism can remain stable under extreme cognitive asymmetry.

This dissertation does not predict that superintelligence will cooperate with humanity. It asks a more tractable question:

\boxed{\text{What conditions make cooperation stable?}}

12.2 The Central Analytical Results

The central analytical results are:

1. The unique positive equilibrium of the bounded two-state system is globally asymptotically stable, proven via Dulac's criterion, the Poincaré–Bendixson theorem, and an explicit boundary analysis.
2. Autonomous coexistence is viable if and only if

r_H\ge r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}=\frac{\lambda\beta U_{\min}}{(1-U_{\min})(\beta+\gamma U_{\min})},

within the reduced autonomous model.

3. The autonomous viability kernel \mathcal{V}_0 is the largest invariant subset of the safe set. Its exact geometry is characterized in Theorem 6.4. In both the positive-margin and zero-margin cases, it has nonempty interior. In the zero-margin case, the equilibrium lies on \partial\mathcal{K}, making viability fragile.
4. The controlled viability kernel \mathcal{V}_u is strictly larger than \mathcal{V}_0 in general; policy can maintain viability even when the autonomous system cannot.
5. The Autonomy Viability Margin \Phi(\rho) provides a scalar summary of whether autonomy remains possible as cognitive asymmetry increases, conditional on an explicit constitutive parameter mapping.
6. Collapse of autonomy occurs not through instability but through the stable equilibrium lying below the viability threshold.

12.3 The Structural Conditions for Coexistence

The theory identifies conditions for autonomous coexistence under extreme cognitive asymmetry at three layers:

Capability layer: r_H\ge r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}.

Structural layer: X_H>0, R>0, M_H>0.

Constitutional layer: U_H\ge U_{\min}^{(c)}.

These are not capability-parity conditions. They do not require humans to match AI in cognitive capability. They require the maintenance of the structural and constitutional conditions under which autonomy is viable.

12.4 The Central Open Question

The central open question is whether the regeneration rate of autonomous human capability can be maintained above the critical threshold as cognitive asymmetry grows without bound. This is an empirical question, not a mathematical one. The theory provides the formal apparatus for analyzing the consequences of different answers, conditional on a specified constitutive mapping from asymmetry to dynamical parameters.

12.5 Final Remark

The theory provides formal, dynamical, and institutional tools for analyzing the conditions under which human autonomy can survive the emergence of superintelligent artificial systems. The central result is that, within the reduced autonomous model, autonomous coexistence is viable if and only if the regeneration rate of autonomous capability meets or exceeds a critical threshold determined by the atrophy coefficient, dependence dynamics, and the autonomy floor.

The system is globally asymptotically stable. Collapse occurs not through instability but through the stable equilibrium lying below the viability threshold. The Autonomy Viability Margin \Phi(\rho) provides a scalar summary of whether autonomy remains possible as cognitive asymmetry increases, conditional on a specified constitutive mapping.

The question is not whether humans can be equal to superintelligence. The question is whether the structural conditions for autonomy can be maintained. The theory provides the formal framework for answering that question.


