Appendix B: Proof of Lemma 4.2 (No Invariant Boundary Components)

Lemma 4.2. The boundary \partial([0,1]^2) contains no invariant subset supporting a nontrivial limiting trajectory. The only invariant subsets of \partial([0,1]^2) are the corners, none of which is invariant under the full flow.

Proof. The boundary \partial([0,1]^2) consists of four edges and four corners. We analyze each.

Edge A_H=0, D_H\in[0,1]. The vector field restricted to this edge is

\dot{A}_H\big|_{A_H=0}=r_H(1-0)-\lambda D_H\cdot 0=r_H>0.

Since \dot{A}_H>0 everywhere on this edge, the vector field points strictly into the interior \{A_H>0\}. Therefore no subset of this edge is forward-invariant. Any trajectory reaching this edge immediately leaves it into the interior.

Edge A_H=1, D_H\in[0,1]. The vector field restricted to this edge is

\dot{A}_H\big|_{A_H=1}=r_H(1-1)-\lambda D_H\cdot 1=-\lambda D_H.

For D_H\in(0,1], \dot{A}_H<0, so the vector field points into the interior \{A_H<1\}. At D_H=0, \dot{A}_H=0 and \dot{D}_H=\beta(1-0)-\gamma\cdot 1\cdot 0=\beta>0, so the vector field points into the interior \{D_H>0\}. Therefore no subset of this edge is forward-invariant.

Edge D_H=0, A_H\in[0,1]. The vector field restricted to this edge is

\dot{D}_H\big|_{D_H=0}=\beta(1-0)-\gamma A_H\cdot 0=\beta>0.

Since \dot{D}_H>0 everywhere on this edge, the vector field points strictly into the interior \{D_H>0\}. Therefore no subset of this edge is forward-invariant.

Edge D_H=1, A_H\in[0,1]. The vector field restricted to this edge is

\dot{D}_H\big|_{D_H=1}=\beta(1-1)-\gamma A_H\cdot 1=-\gamma A_H.

For A_H\in(0,1], \dot{D}_H<0, so the vector field points into the interior \{D_H<1\}. At A_H=0, \dot{D}_H=0 and \dot{A}_H=r_H(1-0)-\lambda\cdot 1\cdot 0=r_H>0, so the vector field points into the interior \{A_H>0\}. Therefore no subset of this edge is forward-invariant.

Corner (0,0). The vector field is

\dot{A}_H=r_H>0,\qquad \dot{D}_H=\beta>0.

The vector field points into the interior. Not invariant.

Corner (1,0). The vector field is

\dot{A}_H=0,\qquad \dot{D}_H=\beta>0.

The vector field points into the interior (in the D_H direction). Not invariant.

Corner (0,1). The vector field is

\dot{A}_H=r_H>0,\qquad \dot{D}_H=0.

The vector field points into the interior (in the A_H direction). Not invariant.

Corner (1,1). The vector field is

\dot{A}_H=-\lambda<0,\qquad \dot{D}_H=-\gamma<0.

The vector field points into the interior (decreasing both coordinates). Not invariant.

Since no edge and no corner is forward-invariant, and the boundary is the union of these edges and corners, no subset of \partial([0,1]^2) is forward-invariant. Therefore no invariant subset of the boundary supports a nontrivial limiting trajectory. \square

Corollary B.1. Every trajectory starting in (0,1)^2 remains in (0,1)^2 for all t\ge 0.

Proof. By Lemma 4.2, the vector field on \partial([0,1]^2) points strictly into the interior, except at isolated non-invariant corners. Since (0,1)^2 is forward-invariant under the flow (the vector field points inward on the boundary), any trajectory starting in the interior remains in the interior for all t\ge 0. \square

