Appendix C: Proof of Theorem 4.3 (Global Asymptotic Stability)

Theorem 4.3. The unique equilibrium (A_H^*,D_H^*) is globally asymptotically stable in (0,1)^2.

Proof. The proof proceeds in four steps: (1) existence and uniqueness of the equilibrium, (2) local asymptotic stability, (3) exclusion of periodic orbits via Dulac's criterion, (4) exclusion of boundary limit sets and application of the Poincaré–Bendixson theorem.

---

Step 1: Existence and Uniqueness of the Equilibrium

At equilibrium, \dot{A}_H=0 and \dot{D}_H=0. From \dot{A}_H=0:

r_H(1-A_H^*)=\lambda D_H^*A_H^*.
\tag{C.1}

From \dot{D}_H=0:

\beta(1-D_H^*)=\gamma A_H^*D_H^*.
\tag{C.2}

From (C.1):

A_H^*=\frac{r_H}{r_H+\lambda D_H^*}.
\tag{C.3}

From (C.2):

D_H^*=\frac{\beta}{\beta+\gamma A_H^*}.
\tag{C.4}

Substituting (C.4) into (C.3):

A_H^*=\frac{r_H}{r_H+\lambda\frac{\beta}{\beta+\gamma A_H^*}}.

Multiplying through by the denominator:

A_H^*\left(r_H+\frac{\lambda\beta}{\beta+\gamma A_H^*}\right)=r_H.

Multiplying by \beta+\gamma A_H^*:

A_H^*\left[r_H(\beta+\gamma A_H^*)+\lambda\beta\right]=r_H(\beta+\gamma A_H^*).

Expanding:

r_H\beta A_H^*+r_H\gamma(A_H^*)^2+\lambda\beta A_H^*=r_H\beta+r_H\gamma A_H^*.

Rearranging:

r_H\gamma(A_H^*)^2+\left[\beta(r_H+\lambda)-r_H\gamma\right]A_H^*-r_H\beta=0.
\tag{C.5}

This is a quadratic in A_H^*:

a(A_H^*)^2+bA_H^*+c=0,

with

a=r_H\gamma>0,\qquad b=\beta(r_H+\lambda)-r_H\gamma,\qquad c=-r_H\beta<0.

Since a>0 and c<0, the product of the roots is c/a<0. Therefore the two roots have opposite signs. There is exactly one positive root:

A_H^*=\frac{-b+\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}
=\frac{-\left[\beta(r_H+\lambda)-r_H\gamma\right]+\sqrt{\left[\beta(r_H+\lambda)-r_H\gamma\right]^2+4r_H^2\beta\gamma}}{2r_H\gamma}.
\tag{C.6}

The corresponding D_H^* is given by (C.4):

D_H^*=\frac{\beta}{\beta+\gamma A_H^*}.
\tag{C.7}

Since A_H^*>0 and \beta,\gamma>0, we have D_H^*\in(0,1). Therefore (A_H^*,D_H^*)\in(0,1)^2. This establishes existence and uniqueness. \square

---

Step 2: Local Asymptotic Stability

The Jacobian of (1.1)–(1.2) at (A_H^*,D_H^*) is

J(A_H^*,D_H^*)=
\begin{pmatrix}
-r_H-\lambda D_H^* & -\lambda A_H^*\\[4pt]
-\gamma D_H^* & -\beta-\gamma A_H^*
\end{pmatrix}.

The trace is

\operatorname{tr}(J)=-(r_H+\lambda D_H^*)-(\beta+\gamma A_H^*)<0.

The determinant is

\det(J)=(r_H+\lambda D_H^*)(\beta+\gamma A_H^*)-\lambda\gamma A_H^*D_H^*.

Expanding:

\det(J)=r_H\beta+r_H\gamma A_H^*+\lambda\beta D_H^*+\lambda\gamma A_H^*D_H^*-\lambda\gamma A_H^*D_H^*.

The last two terms cancel:

\det(J)=r_H\beta+r_H\gamma A_H^*+\lambda\beta D_H^*>0.

Since \operatorname{tr}(J)<0 and \det(J)>0, both eigenvalues of J have negative real part. Therefore (A_H^*,D_H^*) is locally asymptotically stable. \square

---

Step 3: Exclusion of Periodic Orbits via Dulac's Criterion

Define the Dulac multiplier

B(A_H,D_H)=\frac{1}{A_HD_H},

which is C^1 on the interior (0,1)^2. Let F=(F_1,F_2) denote the vector field:

F_1(A_H,D_H)=r_H(1-A_H)-\lambda D_HA_H,

F_2(A_H,D_H)=\beta(1-D_H)-\gamma A_HD_H.

Compute BF:

B F_1=\frac{r_H(1-A_H)}{A_HD_H}-\lambda,

B F_2=\frac{\beta(1-D_H)}{A_HD_H}-\gamma.

Compute the divergence:

\frac{\partial}{\partial A_H}(BF_1)
=
\frac{\partial}{\partial A_H}\left(\frac{r_H(1-A_H)}{A_HD_H}\right)
=
\frac{r_H}{D_H}\cdot\frac{\partial}{\partial A_H}\left(\frac{1-A_H}{A_H}\right).

Now

\frac{\partial}{\partial A_H}\left(\frac{1-A_H}{A_H}\right)
=
\frac{\partial}{\partial A_H}\left(\frac{1}{A_H}-1\right)
=
-\frac{1}{A_H^2}.

Therefore

\frac{\partial}{\partial A_H}(BF_1)
=
-\frac{r_H}{A_H^2D_H}.

Similarly,

\frac{\partial}{\partial D_H}(BF_2)
=
\frac{\partial}{\partial D_H}\left(\frac{\beta(1-D_H)}{A_HD_H}\right)
=
\frac{\beta}{A_H}\cdot\frac{\partial}{\partial D_H}\left(\frac{1-D_H}{D_H}\right).

Now

\frac{\partial}{\partial D_H}\left(\frac{1-D_H}{D_H}\right)
=
\frac{\partial}{\partial D_H}\left(\frac{1}{D_H}-1\right)
=
-\frac{1}{D_H^2}.

Therefore

\frac{\partial}{\partial D_H}(BF_2)
=
-\frac{\beta}{A_HD_H^2}.

Thus

\nabla\cdot(BF)
=
-\frac{r_H}{A_H^2D_H}-\frac{\beta}{A_HD_H^2}<0

for all (A_H,D_H)\in(0,1)^2. Since the divergence has constant sign (strictly negative) on the simply connected domain (0,1)^2, Dulac's criterion implies that the system has no periodic orbits, no homoclinic cycles, and no heteroclinic cycles in (0,1)^2. \square

---

Step 4: Exclusion of Boundary Limit Sets and Application of Poincaré–Bendixson

By Lemma 4.2 and Corollary B.1, the domain (0,1)^2 is forward-invariant, and no subset of \partial([0,1]^2) is invariant. Therefore, for any x_0\in(0,1)^2, the forward trajectory \phi_t(x_0) remains in (0,1)^2 for all t\ge 0.

The trajectory is contained in the compact set [0,1]^2, so its \omega-limit set \omega(x_0) is nonempty, compact, and invariant. Since the trajectory remains in (0,1)^2, we have \omega(x_0)\subseteq\overline{(0,1)^2}=[0,1]^2. But by Lemma 4.2, \omega(x_0) cannot intersect \partial([0,1]^2) (since the boundary contains no invariant sets). Therefore \omega(x_0)\subseteq(0,1)^2.

By the Poincaré–Bendixson theorem, a nonempty compact invariant limit set in the plane that contains no equilibria must be a periodic orbit. But Step 3 (Dulac's criterion) excludes periodic orbits in (0,1)^2. Therefore \omega(x_0) must contain at least one equilibrium.

By Proposition 4.1, the only equilibrium in (0,1)^2 is (A_H^*,D_H^*). Therefore \omega(x_0)=\{(A_H^*,D_H^*)\}. Since x_0 was arbitrary, every trajectory in (0,1)^2 converges to (A_H^*,D_H^*).

Combined with local asymptotic stability (Step 2), this establishes global asymptotic stability. \square

---

Remark C.1

The Dulac argument alone excludes periodic orbits but does not by itself establish convergence. The Poincaré–Bendixson theorem, combined with the boundary analysis of Lemma 4.2, supplies the missing step. Without the boundary analysis, a trajectory could in principle converge to a limit set on the boundary; Lemma 4.2 excludes this possibility.

Remark C.2

The Dulac multiplier B=1/(A_HD_H) is not C^1 on the boundary \partial([0,1]^2). The Dulac criterion is applied on the interior (0,1)^2, which is simply connected and forward-invariant. This is sufficient.

Corollary C.3

The basin of attraction of (A_H^*,D_H^*) is all of (0,1)^2:

\mathcal{B}(A_H^*,D_H^*)=(0,1)^2.

---

Appendix D: Proof of Theorem 5.3 (Autonomous Viability Theorem)

Theorem 5.3. Let the autonomous system (1.1)–(1.2) be defined on [0,1]^2 with r_H,\lambda,\beta,\gamma>0 and U_{\min}\in(0,1). Then:

(A) If r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, the equilibrium satisfies A_H^*>U_{\min}, and \mathcal{V}_0\neq\varnothing with nonempty interior.

(B) If r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, the equilibrium satisfies A_H^*=U_{\min}, and \mathcal{V}_0\neq\varnothing with nonempty interior in \mathcal{K}. The equilibrium lies on the boundary of the safe set.

(C) If r_H<r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, the equilibrium satisfies A_H^*<U_{\min}, and \mathcal{V}_0=\varnothing.

Proof.

---

Preliminary: The Critical Threshold

The critical threshold is defined by

r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}=\frac{\lambda\beta U_{\min}}{(1-U_{\min})(\beta+\gamma U_{\min})}.
\tag{D.1}

Equivalently, the viability condition is

r_H(1-U_{\min})(\beta+\gamma U_{\min})\ge\lambda\beta U_{\min}.
\tag{D.2}

We first establish the equivalence between (D.2) and the condition A_H^*\ge U_{\min}.

From the equilibrium condition (C.1):

r_H(1-A_H^*)=\lambda D_H^*A_H^*.

At A_H^*=U_{\min}, this gives

r_H(1-U_{\min})=\lambda D_H^*U_{\min},

so

D_H^*=\frac{r_H(1-U_{\min})}{\lambda U_{\min}}=D_{\mathrm{crit}}.
\tag{D.3}

From the equilibrium condition (C.2):

\beta(1-D_H^*)=\gamma U_{\min}D_H^*.

Substituting (D.3):

\beta\left(1-\frac{r_H(1-U_{\min})}{\lambda U_{\min}}\right)
=
\gamma U_{\min}\frac{r_H(1-U_{\min})}{\lambda U_{\min}}.

Multiplying through by \lambda U_{\min}:

\beta(\lambda U_{\min}-r_H(1-U_{\min}))=\gamma r_H U_{\min}(1-U_{\min}).

Rearranging:

\lambda\beta U_{\min}=r_H(1-U_{\min})(\beta+\gamma U_{\min}).

This is exactly (D.2) with equality. Therefore A_H^*=U_{\min} if and only if r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}.

Since A_H^* is increasing in r_H (from the equilibrium relations (C.3)–(C.4)), we have:

r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}\iff A_H^*>U_{\min},

r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}\iff A_H^*=U_{\min},

r_H<r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}\iff A_H^*<U_{\min}.

---

Proof of (A): r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}

Suppose r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}. Then A_H^*>U_{\min}. The equilibrium (A_H^*,D_H^*) lies in the interior of \mathcal{K}:

(A_H^*,D_H^*)\in\mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K})=\{(A_H,D_H):A_H>U_{\min}\}.

Since (A_H^*,D_H^*) is globally asymptotically stable (Theorem 4.3), there exists \epsilon>0 such that the ball B_\epsilon(A_H^*,D_H^*)\subseteq\mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K}) and every trajectory starting in this ball converges to (A_H^*,D_H^*) without leaving \mathcal{K}. Therefore B_\epsilon(A_H^*,D_H^*)\subseteq\mathcal{V}_0, so \mathcal{V}_0 has nonempty interior. \square

---

Proof of (B): r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}

Suppose r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}. Then A_H^*=U_{\min} and D_H^*=D_{\mathrm{crit}}. The equilibrium (U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}}) lies on the boundary \partial\mathcal{K}=\{A_H=U_{\min}\}.

First, \mathcal{V}_0\neq\varnothing because the equilibrium itself is in \mathcal{V}_0 (it is a fixed point satisfying A_H=U_{\min}\ge U_{\min}).

Second, we show that \mathcal{V}_0 has nonempty interior in \mathcal{K}. Consider the point (U_{\min},D_H) with D_H\in[0,D_{\mathrm{crit}}). At this point:

\dot{A}_H\big|_{A_H=U_{\min}}
=
r_H(1-U_{\min})-\lambda D_HU_{\min}.

Since D_H<D_{\mathrm{crit}}=r_H(1-U_{\min})/(\lambda U_{\min}), we have

\lambda D_HU_{\min}<\lambda D_{\mathrm{crit}}U_{\min}=r_H(1-U_{\min}),

so

\dot{A}_H>0.

Therefore the trajectory starting at (U_{\min},D_H) enters the interior \{A_H>U_{\min}\} immediately. By Theorem 4.3, the trajectory converges to (U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}}). Since it enters the interior and the equilibrium is on the boundary, the trajectory must approach the equilibrium from the interior. Therefore it remains in \mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0, so (U_{\min},D_H)\in\mathcal{V}_0 for all D_H\in[0,D_{\mathrm{crit}}).

By continuity of the flow with respect to initial conditions, there exists a neighborhood N of the segment \{(U_{\min},D_H):0\le D_H<D_{\mathrm{crit}}\} in \mathcal{K} such that every point in N has a trajectory that remains in \mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0. Therefore N\subseteq\mathcal{V}_0, so \mathcal{V}_0 has nonempty interior in \mathcal{K}. \square

---

Proof of (C): r_H<r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}

Suppose r_H<r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}. Then A_H^*<U_{\min}. The equilibrium (A_H^*,D_H^*) lies strictly below the safe set:

(A_H^*,D_H^*)\in\{(A_H,D_H):A_H<U_{\min}\}.

By Theorem 4.3, every trajectory in (0,1)^2 converges to (A_H^*,D_H^*). Since A_H^*<U_{\min}, every trajectory must cross the line A_H=U_{\min} at some finite time t_c<\infty. At the crossing time, A_H(t_c)=U_{\min} and for t>t_c sufficiently close, A_H(t)<U_{\min}. Therefore the trajectory leaves \mathcal{K} and does not remain in \mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0.

Since this holds for every initial condition in \mathcal{K}, there is no initial condition in \mathcal{K} from which the trajectory remains in \mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0. Therefore \mathcal{V}_0=\varnothing. \square

---

Corollary D.1

\mathcal{V}_0\neq\varnothing\iff r_H\ge r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}.

---

Appendix E: Proof of Theorem 6.4 (Exact Autonomous Viability Kernel)

Theorem 6.4. Let r_H\ge r_H^{\mathrm{crit}} and U_{\min}\in(0,1). Then:

\mathcal{V}_0=\{(A_H,D_H)\in\mathcal{K}:\phi_t(A_H,D_H)\in\mathcal{K}\ \forall t\ge 0\},

and \mathcal{V}_0 has the following structure:

(i) \mathcal{V}_0 is closed in [0,1]^2, forward-invariant, and contains the equilibrium (A_H^*,D_H^*).

(ii) The segment \Sigma_0=\{(U_{\min},D_H):0\le D_H\le D_{\mathrm{crit}}\} is contained in \partial\mathcal{V}_0\cap\partial\mathcal{K}.

(iii) The backward branch \Gamma_- of the critical trajectory is contained in \partial\mathcal{V}_0\cap\mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K}) and forms the upper boundary of \mathcal{V}_0 within the interior. (In the critical case, the boundary is the separatrix; see (v).)

(iv) When r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, \mathcal{V}_0 has nonempty interior in \mathcal{K}, contains an open neighborhood of (A_H^*,D_H^*), and

\mathcal{V}_0=\mathcal{K}\setminus\mathcal{E},

where \mathcal{E} is the open set of initial conditions whose trajectories cross \{A_H=U_{\min},D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}}\}.

(v) When r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, \mathcal{V}_0 has nonempty interior in \mathcal{K}. The equilibrium E^*=(U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}}) lies on \partial\mathcal{K}. The boundary of \mathcal{V}_0 within \mathcal{K} consists of the segment \Sigma_0 on \partial\mathcal{K} together with a separatrix curve S in \mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K}) that approaches E^* tangentially and forms the upper boundary of \mathcal{V}_0.

Proof.

---

Proof of (i): Closedness, Forward Invariance, and Containment of the Equilibrium

Closedness. The set \mathcal{V}_0 is defined as

\mathcal{V}_0=\{x\in\mathcal{K}:\phi_t(x)\in\mathcal{K}\ \forall t\ge 0\}.

Let \{x_n\}_{n=1}^\infty\subseteq\mathcal{V}_0 be a sequence converging to x\in\mathcal{K}. For each n and each t\ge 0, \phi_t(x_n)\in\mathcal{K}. Since the flow \phi_t is continuous in x for each fixed t, we have \phi_t(x_n)\to\phi_t(x) as n\to\infty. Since \mathcal{K} is closed, \phi_t(x)\in\mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0. Therefore x\in\mathcal{V}_0, so \mathcal{V}_0 is closed.

Forward invariance. If x\in\mathcal{V}_0, then \phi_t(x)\in\mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0. For any s\ge 0, the trajectory starting at \phi_s(x) is \phi_{t+s}(x), which is in \mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0. Therefore \phi_s(x)\in\mathcal{V}_0. Hence \mathcal{V}_0 is forward-invariant.

Containment of the equilibrium. The equilibrium (A_H^*,D_H^*) satisfies A_H^*\ge U_{\min} (by Theorem 5.3(A)–(B)). Since it is a fixed point, \phi_t(A_H^*,D_H^*)=(A_H^*,D_H^*)\in\mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0. Therefore (A_H^*,D_H^*)\in\mathcal{V}_0. \square

---

Proof of (ii): The Segment \Sigma_0

Let D_H\in[0,D_{\mathrm{crit}}). At (U_{\min},D_H):

\dot{A}_H=r_H(1-U_{\min})-\lambda D_HU_{\min}.

Since D_H<D_{\mathrm{crit}}=r_H(1-U_{\min})/(\lambda U_{\min}), we have \dot{A}_H>0. Therefore the trajectory enters \{A_H>U_{\min}\} immediately.

By Theorem 4.3, the trajectory converges to (A_H^*,D_H^*). Since A_H^*\ge U_{\min} (with equality in the critical case), the trajectory remains in \mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0. Therefore (U_{\min},D_H)\in\mathcal{V}_0.

For D_H=D_{\mathrm{crit}}, (U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}}) is the equilibrium (in the critical case) or a point on the critical trajectory (in the positive-margin case). In either case, it is in \mathcal{V}_0.

To show that \Sigma_0\subseteq\partial\mathcal{V}_0: Consider a point (U_{\min},D_H) with D_H\in[0,D_{\mathrm{crit}}). Any neighborhood of this point in \mathbb{R}^2 contains points with A_H<U_{\min}, which are not in \mathcal{K} and hence not in \mathcal{V}_0. Therefore (U_{\min},D_H)\in\partial\mathcal{V}_0.

For D_H\in(D_{\mathrm{crit}},1], at (U_{\min},D_H), \dot{A}_H<0, so the trajectory exits \mathcal{K} immediately. Therefore these points are not in \mathcal{V}_0. \square

---

Proof of (iii): The Critical Trajectory

Positive-margin case (r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}). The critical trajectory \Gamma passes through (U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}}). At this point, \dot{A}_H=0 and \dot{D}_H<0. The forward branch \Gamma_+ enters \{A_H>U_{\min}\} (by Lemma 6.2) and converges to (A_H^*,D_H^*). The backward branch \Gamma_- enters \{A_H>U_{\min}\}\cap\{D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}}\} and extends to \partial([0,1]^2) (by Lemma 6.3).

Points on \Gamma_- are in \mathcal{V}_0 because their forward trajectories pass through (U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}}) and then remain in \mathcal{K}. Points in a neighborhood of \Gamma_- on the "upper" side (larger D_H for fixed A_H) have trajectories that cross \{A_H=U_{\min},D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}}\} and exit \mathcal{K}. Therefore \Gamma_-\subseteq\partial\mathcal{V}_0\cap\mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K}).

Critical case (r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}). The critical trajectory is degenerate: \Gamma(t)=(U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}})=E^* for all t. The boundary of \mathcal{V}_0 within \mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K}) is the separatrix S, analyzed in (v) below. \square

---

Proof of (iv): Positive-Margin Case

Suppose r_H>r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}. Then A_H^*>U_{\min}, so the equilibrium is strictly interior to \mathcal{K}.

Nonempty interior. Since (A_H^*,D_H^*)\in\mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K}) and the equilibrium is globally asymptotically stable, there exists \epsilon>0 such that B_\epsilon(A_H^*,D_H^*)\subseteq\mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K}) and every trajectory starting in this ball converges to (A_H^*,D_H^*) without leaving \mathcal{K}. Therefore B_\epsilon(A_H^*,D_H^*)\subseteq\mathcal{V}_0, so \mathcal{V}_0 has nonempty interior.

Characterization of \mathcal{V}_0. Define

\mathcal{E}=\{x\in\mathcal{K}:\exists t>0\text{ such that }A_H(t)=U_{\min}\text{ and }D_H(t)>D_{\mathrm{crit}}\}.

That is, \mathcal{E} is the set of initial conditions whose trajectories cross the exit segment \{A_H=U_{\min},D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}}\}.

We claim that \mathcal{V}_0=\mathcal{K}\setminus\mathcal{E}.

First, suppose x\in\mathcal{V}_0. Then \phi_t(x)\in\mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0, so the trajectory never crosses \{A_H=U_{\min},D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}}\} (since crossing would immediately take the trajectory into \{A_H<U_{\min}\}). Therefore x\notin\mathcal{E}. Hence \mathcal{V}_0\subseteq\mathcal{K}\setminus\mathcal{E}.

Conversely, suppose x\in\mathcal{K}\setminus\mathcal{E}. Then the trajectory never crosses the exit segment. Since the trajectory is continuous and starts in \mathcal{K}, and the only way to leave \mathcal{K} is to cross \{A_H=U_{\min},D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}}\}, the trajectory remains in \mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0. Therefore x\in\mathcal{V}_0. Hence \mathcal{K}\setminus\mathcal{E}\subseteq\mathcal{V}_0.

Therefore \mathcal{V}_0=\mathcal{K}\setminus\mathcal{E}. \square

---

Proof of (v): Critical Case

Suppose r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}. Then A_H^*=U_{\min} and D_H^*=D_{\mathrm{crit}}. The equilibrium E^*=(U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}}) lies on \partial\mathcal{K}.

Nonempty interior. We established in the proof of Theorem 5.3(B) that \mathcal{V}_0 contains a neighborhood of the segment \{(U_{\min},D_H):0\le D_H<D_{\mathrm{crit}}\} in \mathcal{K}. Therefore \mathcal{V}_0 has nonempty interior in \mathcal{K}.

The separatrix. Consider the linearization of the system at E^*. Let x=A_H-U_{\min}, y=D_H-D_{\mathrm{crit}}. The linearized system is

\begin{pmatrix}\dot{x}\\\dot{y}\end{pmatrix}
=
J
\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\end{pmatrix},

where

J=
\begin{pmatrix}
-(r_H+\lambda D_{\mathrm{crit}}) & -\lambda U_{\min}\\[4pt]
-\gamma D_{\mathrm{crit}} & -(\beta+\gamma U_{\min})
\end{pmatrix}.

Let

a=r_H+\lambda D_{\mathrm{crit}}>0,\quad b=\lambda U_{\min}>0,\quad c=\gamma D_{\mathrm{crit}}>0,\quad d=\beta+\gamma U_{\min}>0.

Then

J=\begin{pmatrix}-a&-b\\-c&-d\end{pmatrix}.

The trace is \operatorname{tr}(J)=-(a+d)<0. The determinant is

\det(J)=ad-bc=(r_H+\lambda D_{\mathrm{crit}})(\beta+\gamma U_{\min})-\lambda\gamma U_{\min}D_{\mathrm{crit}}.

Expanding:

\det(J)=r_H\beta+r_H\gamma U_{\min}+\lambda\beta D_{\mathrm{crit}}+\lambda\gamma U_{\min}D_{\mathrm{crit}}-\lambda\gamma U_{\min}D_{\mathrm{crit}}
=r_H\beta+r_H\gamma U_{\min}+\lambda\beta D_{\mathrm{crit}}>0.

Therefore both eigenvalues \lambda_1,\lambda_2 of J are negative (stable node). Let \lambda_s be the eigenvalue with smaller magnitude (slow eigenvalue) and \lambda_f the one with larger magnitude (fast eigenvalue).

The eigenvectors satisfy Jv=\lambda v. For the slow eigenvalue \lambda_s:

(-a-\lambda_s)v_1-bv_2=0,

so

v_2=\frac{-a-\lambda_s}{b}v_1.

Since \lambda_s<0 and |\lambda_s|<a (as shown in Appendix F), we have -a-\lambda_s<0. Taking v_1>0, we get v_2<0. Therefore the slow eigenvector points into the region \{x>0,y<0\}, i.e., into \mathcal{K} (toward larger A_H, smaller D_H).

By the stable manifold theorem, there exists a unique trajectory S, called the separatrix, that approaches E^* tangentially to the slow eigenvector. The separatrix is a C^1 curve in \mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K})\cup\{E^*\} that divides \mathcal{K} into two regions:

· The region containing \Sigma_0=\{(U_{\min},D_H):0\le D_H<D_{\mathrm{crit}}\}, denoted \mathcal{V}_0^+.
· The region containing the exit segment \{(U_{\min},D_H):D_{\mathrm{crit}}<D_H\le 1\}, denoted \mathcal{V}_0^-.

Trajectories starting in \mathcal{V}_0^+ approach E^* from within \mathcal{K} without crossing the exit segment. Trajectories starting in \mathcal{V}_0^- cross the exit segment and leave \mathcal{K}.

Therefore \mathcal{V}_0=\mathcal{V}_0^+, and the boundary of \mathcal{V}_0 within \mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K}) is the separatrix S. \square

---

Lemma E.1 (Properties of the Separatrix)

The separatrix S in the critical case has the following properties:

(i) S is a C^1 curve in \mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K})\cup\{E^*\}.

(ii) S approaches E^* tangentially to the slow eigenvector v_s.

(iii) S is invariant under the flow.

(iv) S divides \mathcal{K} into \mathcal{V}_0 and \mathcal{K}\setminus\mathcal{V}_0.

Proof.

(i) This follows from the stable manifold theorem applied to the hyperbolic equilibrium E^*. The weak stable manifold W^s_{\mathrm{weak}}(E^*) is a 1D C^1 manifold.

(ii) By definition of the weak stable manifold, trajectories on W^s_{\mathrm{weak}}(E^*) approach E^* tangentially to the slow eigenvector.

(iii) The stable manifold is invariant under the flow by definition.

(iv) Since W^s_{\mathrm{weak}}(E^*) is 1D and separates the plane locally near E^*, and since the only way to leave \mathcal{K} is to cross the exit segment, the branch of W^s_{\mathrm{weak}}(E^*) in \mathcal{K} separates \mathcal{V}_0 from \mathcal{K}\setminus\mathcal{V}_0. \square

---

Remark E.2

In the critical case, the statement in earlier versions that \mathcal{V}_0=\Gamma_-\cup\{E^*\}\cup\Gamma_+ is incorrect. The critical trajectory through E^* is degenerate (it is the fixed point E^*). The correct boundary of \mathcal{V}_0 within \mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K}) is the separatrix S, which is a nontrivial curve approaching E^* tangentially.

Remark E.3

The zero-margin case r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}} represents a fragile viability state: the equilibrium is viable but lies on \partial\mathcal{K}. Any perturbation that pushes the state into \{A_H<U_{\min}\} leads to exit. The viability kernel has nonempty interior, but the equilibrium is not robustly viable.

---

Appendix F: Analysis of the Slow Eigenvalue and Eigenvector

Lemma F.1. For the Jacobian

J=\begin{pmatrix}-a&-b\\-c&-d\end{pmatrix}

with a,b,c,d>0 and ad-bc>0, the eigenvalues \lambda_s,\lambda_f satisfy \lambda_f<\lambda_s<0 and |\lambda_s|<a.

Proof. The eigenvalues are

\lambda_{s,f}=\frac{-(a+d)\pm\sqrt{(a+d)^2-4(ad-bc)}}{2}.

Since ad-bc>0, the discriminant is

\Delta=(a+d)^2-4(ad-bc)=a^2+2ad+d^2-4ad+4bc=a^2-2ad+d^2+4bc=(a-d)^2+4bc>0.

Therefore the eigenvalues are real and distinct. Both are negative since \operatorname{tr}(J)=-(a+d)<0 and \det(J)=ad-bc>0.

Let \lambda_s be the eigenvalue with smaller magnitude:

\lambda_s=\frac{-(a+d)+\sqrt{\Delta}}{2}.

We show |\lambda_s|<a, i.e., -a<\lambda_s<0.

Since \lambda_s<0, we need \lambda_s>-a. This is equivalent to

\frac{-(a+d)+\sqrt{\Delta}}{2}>-a
\iff -(a+d)+\sqrt{\Delta}>-2a
\iff \sqrt{\Delta}>d-a.

If d\le a, this is trivially true since \sqrt{\Delta}>0\ge d-a. If d>a, we square both sides:

\Delta>(d-a)^2
\iff (a-d)^2+4bc>(d-a)^2
\iff 4bc>0.

This is true since b,c>0. Therefore |\lambda_s|<a. \square

Corollary F.2. The slow eigenvector v_s=(v_1,v_2) satisfies v_2/v_1=(-a-\lambda_s)/b<0. Therefore the slow eigenvector points into the region \{x>0,y<0\} (larger A_H, smaller D_H).

Proof. From Jv_s=\lambda_sv_s:

(-a-\lambda_s)v_1-bv_2=0\implies v_2=\frac{-a-\lambda_s}{b}v_1.

Since |\lambda_s|<a, we have -a-\lambda_s<0. With b>0, v_2/v_1<0. Taking v_1>0, we get v_2<0. \square

Corollary F.3. The fast eigenvector v_f=(v_1,v_2) satisfies v_2/v_1=(-a-\lambda_f)/b>0.

Proof. Since |\lambda_f|>a (as shown in Appendix F of the main text), we have -a-\lambda_f>0. With b>0, v_2/v_1>0. Taking v_1>0, we get v_2>0. \square

Remark F.4. The fast eigenvector points into the region \{x>0,y>0\} (larger A_H, larger D_H), which is the region \mathcal{R}=\{A_H>U_{\min},D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}}\}. Trajectories approaching E^* along the fast eigenvector come from \mathcal{R} and have crossed the exit segment (or will cross it). Trajectories approaching E^* along the slow eigenvector come from \mathcal{K} and have not crossed the exit segment.

---

Appendix G: Dulac Criterion and Poincaré–Bendixson Theorem

G.1 Dulac's Criterion

Theorem G.1 (Dulac's Criterion). Let D\subseteq\mathbb{R}^2 be a simply connected domain, and let F:D\to\mathbb{R}^2 be a C^1 vector field. If there exists a C^1 function B:D\to\mathbb{R} such that

\nabla\cdot(BF)

has constant sign (strictly positive or strictly negative) on D, then the system \dot{x}=F(x) has no periodic orbits, no homoclinic cycles, and no heteroclinic cycles in D.

Proof. Suppose \gamma is a periodic orbit in D, enclosing a region R\subseteq D. By the divergence theorem:

\iint_R\nabla\cdot(BF)\,dA=\oint_\gamma BF\cdot n\,ds,

where n is the outward normal to \gamma. Since \gamma is a trajectory of F, F is tangent to \gamma, so F\cdot n=0 on \gamma. Therefore the line integral vanishes:

\iint_R\nabla\cdot(BF)\,dA=0.

But if \nabla\cdot(BF) has constant sign on D, the double integral is strictly positive or strictly negative, a contradiction. Therefore no periodic orbit exists. The same argument applies to homoclinic and heteroclinic cycles (which also enclose regions). \square

G.2 Poincaré–Bendixson Theorem

Theorem G.2 (Poincaré–Bendixson). Let F:\mathbb{R}^2\to\mathbb{R}^2 be a C^1 vector field. If a forward trajectory \phi_t(x_0) is bounded and its \omega-limit set \omega(x_0) contains no equilibria, then \omega(x_0) is a periodic orbit.

Proof. See standard references (e.g., Guckenheimer & Holmes, 1983; Hirsch, Smale & Devaney, 2012). \square

G.3 Combined Application

In the present case:

· The domain D=(0,1)^2 is simply connected.
· The Dulac multiplier B=1/(A_HD_H) gives \nabla\cdot(BF)<0 everywhere in D.
· By Dulac's criterion, there are no periodic orbits in D.
· The domain D is forward-invariant (Corollary B.1).
· The unique equilibrium in D is (A_H^*,D_H^*) (Proposition 4.1).
· For any x_0\in D, the forward trajectory is bounded (contained in [0,1]^2).
· The \omega-limit set \omega(x_0) is nonempty, compact, and invariant.
· Since \omega(x_0)\subseteq D (by Lemma 4.2) and contains no periodic orbits (by Dulac), it must contain an equilibrium.
· The only equilibrium in D is (A_H^*,D_H^*).
· Therefore \omega(x_0)=\{(A_H^*,D_H^*)\}.

This establishes global convergence to the unique equilibrium.

