Appendix I: Proof of Proposition 7.2 (Controlled Viability Kernel Contains Autonomous Viability Kernel)

Proposition 7.2. \mathcal{V}_0\subseteq\mathcal{V}_u.

Proof. Let x\in\mathcal{V}_0. By definition, the autonomous trajectory \phi_t(x) satisfies \phi_t(x)\in\mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0. Consider the constant control u(t)\equiv u_0 for some fixed u_0\in\mathcal{U}. Under this control, the controlled trajectory coincides with the autonomous trajectory (since the parameters are constant). Therefore the controlled trajectory remains in \mathcal{K} for all t\ge 0. Hence x\in\mathcal{V}_u. Therefore \mathcal{V}_0\subseteq\mathcal{V}_u. \square

Remark I.1. The inclusion is strict in general. If r_H<r_H^{\mathrm{crit}} for the autonomous system, a control u(t) may still maintain viability by increasing r_H(u), decreasing \lambda(u), or otherwise altering the parameters. The precise characterization of when the inclusion is strict is an open problem (§11.3).

