Appendix J: Proof of Proposition 8.2 (Scaling Analysis)

Proposition 8.2. Suppose r_H(\rho)\le M<\infty for all \rho, while \lambda(\rho)\to\infty. If \beta(\rho) and \gamma(\rho) remain bounded below by positive constants \beta_0,\gamma_0>0, then \Phi(\rho)<0 for sufficiently large \rho.

Proof. The Autonomy Viability Margin is

\Phi(\rho)=r_H(\rho)(1-U_{\min})[\beta(\rho)+\gamma(\rho)U_{\min}]-\lambda(\rho)\beta(\rho)U_{\min}.

The positive term is bounded above:

r_H(\rho)(1-U_{\min})[\beta(\rho)+\gamma(\rho)U_{\min}]\le M(1-U_{\min})[\beta(\rho)+\gamma(\rho)U_{\min}].

If \beta(\rho) and \gamma(\rho) are bounded above as well (which is typically assumed), the positive term is bounded. The negative term is

-\lambda(\rho)\beta(\rho)U_{\min}\le-\lambda(\rho)\beta_0U_{\min}\to-\infty

as \rho\to\infty. Therefore \Phi(\rho)\to-\infty, so \Phi(\rho)<0 for sufficiently large \rho. \square

Remark J.1. If \beta(\rho)\to 0 sufficiently fast, the divergence of \lambda(\rho) may be offset. The scaling assumptions must be stated jointly for all four parameters. For example, if \lambda(\rho)\beta(\rho)\to 0 as \rho\to\infty, the negative term may remain bounded.

