Appendix M: Numerical Illustration of the Critical Case

To illustrate the geometry of the viability kernel in the critical case, consider the parameter values:

r_H=0.5,\quad \lambda=1.0,\quad \beta=0.4,\quad \gamma=0.6,\quad U_{\min}=0.5.

Compute r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}:

r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}=\frac{\lambda\beta U_{\min}}{(1-U_{\min})(\beta+\gamma U_{\min})}
=\frac{1.0\cdot 0.4\cdot 0.5}{0.5\cdot(0.4+0.6\cdot 0.5)}
=\frac{0.2}{0.5\cdot 0.7}
=\frac{0.2}{0.35}\approx 0.5714.

Since r_H=0.5<0.5714=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}, this is the non-viable case. Adjust r_H to 0.5714 for the critical case:

r_H=0.5714,\quad \lambda=1.0,\quad \beta=0.4,\quad \gamma=0.6,\quad U_{\min}=0.5.

Compute D_{\mathrm{crit}}:

D_{\mathrm{crit}}=\frac{r_H(1-U_{\min})}{\lambda U_{\min}}
=\frac{0.5714\cdot 0.5}{1.0\cdot 0.5}=0.5714.

Compute D_H^*:

D_H^*=\frac{\beta}{\beta+\gamma U_{\min}}
=\frac{0.4}{0.4+0.6\cdot 0.5}
=\frac{0.4}{0.7}\approx 0.5714.

So D_H^*=D_{\mathrm{crit}}, as expected. The equilibrium is E^*=(0.5,0.5714).

The separatrix approaches E^* tangentially to the slow eigenvector. The slow eigenvector points into \mathcal{K} (toward larger A_H, smaller D_H). The viability kernel \mathcal{V}_0 is the region of \mathcal{K} below the separatrix, containing the segment \Sigma_0=\{(0.5,D_H):0\le D_H<0.5714\}.

