Appendix P: Open Problems (Expanded)

P.1 Vector Viability Kernel

The scalar asymmetry summary \rho is a normative construct. The full vector \boldsymbol{\rho}\in\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^n would require a higher-dimensional analysis. The viability kernel for the vector-valued system would be a subset of [0,1]^{2+n}, and its characterization would require methods from higher-dimensional viability theory.

P.2 Stochastic Dynamics

Adding environmental shocks \epsilon(t) to the dynamics:

\dot{A}_H=r_H(1-A_H)-\lambda D_HA_H+\epsilon_1(t),

\dot{D}_H=\beta(1-D_H)-\gamma A_HD_H+\epsilon_2(t).

The stochastic viability kernel is the set of initial states from which there exists a policy such that the probability of remaining in \mathcal{K} exceeds a specified threshold. This requires solving a stochastic control problem.

P.3 Exact Controlled Viability Kernel

Characterize precisely when \mathcal{V}_0\subsetneq\mathcal{V}_u. This requires solving the control barrier function problem:

\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\left[r_H(u)(1-A_H)-\lambda(u)D_HA_H+\alpha(A_H-U_{\min})\right]\ge 0.

The solution would identify the maximal controlled invariant set and the optimal control policy.

P.4 Game-Theoretic Integration

Embed the dynamical system in a repeated game with incomplete information. The players are humans and AI systems (or their operators). The equilibrium governance architectures would be characterized by the Nash equilibria of the repeated game, with the dynamical system as the state evolution.

P.5 Empirical Estimation of Parameters

Estimate r_H,\lambda,\beta,\gamma from longitudinal data on AI adoption, cognitive offloading, and institutional resilience. The current calibration is qualitative. A quantitative calibration would require:

· Time-series data on AI usage and independent performance.
· Natural experiments on AI removal (e.g., the colonoscopy study).
· Cross-country data on institutional resilience and AI adoption.

P.6 Constitutive Mapping Identification

Identify the mapping \rho\mapsto(r_H,\lambda,\beta,\gamma) empirically. This is the central open problem connecting the mathematical model to real-world predictions. Candidates for the mapping include:

· Linear: r_H(\rho)=r_0-k_r\rho, \lambda(\rho)=\lambda_0+k_\lambda\rho.
· Power-law: r_H(\rho)=r_0\rho^{-\alpha}, \lambda(\rho)=\lambda_0\rho^{\beta}.
· Exponential: r_H(\rho)=r_0e^{-\alpha\rho}, \lambda(\rho)=\lambda_0e^{\beta\rho}.

The choice of mapping has significant implications for the limit \liminf_{\rho\to\infty}\Phi(\rho).

P.7 Normative Weights

Justify the weights w_i in the scalar asymmetry summary \rho=\sum_iw_i\rho_i. The weights are normative and cannot be derived from the mathematical model alone. They require deliberative or constitutional procedures.

P.8 Constitutional Autonomy

Develop a formal theory of constitutional autonomy U_H that is independent of capability autonomy A_H and structural autonomy (X_H,R,M_H). This requires a theory of legitimacy and contestability. Candidates for formalization include:

· Social choice theory: U_H as the capacity to influence collective decisions.
· Contestability theory: U_H as the capacity to challenge AI-generated decisions.
· Republican theory: U_H as the absence of domination by AI systems.

P.9 Global Stability Under Control

Prove global asymptotic stability for the controlled system:

\dot{A}_H=r_H(u)(1-A_H)-\lambda(u)D_HA_H,

\dot{D}_H=\beta(u)(1-D_H)-\gamma(u)A_HD_H.

The current global stability result applies to the autonomous system. The controlled system requires a separate analysis, possibly using Lyapunov functions or control barrier functions.

P.10 The Boundary Case r_H=r_H^{\mathrm{crit}}

Analyze the behavior of the system at the critical boundary in the presence of noise and finite-time perturbations. The equilibrium lies on \partial\mathcal{K}, and the viability kernel has nonempty interior. Perturbations may push the system into the non-viable region. The robustness of the viability kernel to perturbations is an open problem.

