Appendix U: Proof of Lemma 6.2 (Forward Branch of the Critical Trajectory)

Lemma 6.2. The forward branch \Gamma_+ is contained in \mathcal{K} and converges to the equilibrium (A_H^*,D_H^*).

Proof. At t=0, \Gamma(0)=(U_{\min},D_{\mathrm{crit}}). The velocity is

\dot{A}_H\big|_{\Gamma(0)}=r_H(1-U_{\min})-\lambda D_{\mathrm{crit}}U_{\min}=0,

\dot{D}_H\big|_{\Gamma(0)}=\beta(1-D_{\mathrm{crit}})-\gamma U_{\min}D_{\mathrm{crit}}.

In the positive-margin case, D_{\mathrm{crit}}<D_H^*, so \dot{D}_H<0. In the critical case, D_{\mathrm{crit}}=D_H^*, so \dot{D}_H=0.

The second-order derivative of A_H along \Gamma at t=0 is

\ddot{A}_H\big|_{\Gamma(0)}
=-(r_H+\lambda D_{\mathrm{crit}})\dot{A}_H-\lambda U_{\min}\dot{D}_H
=-\lambda U_{\min}\dot{D}_H.

In the positive-margin case, \dot{D}_H<0, so \ddot{A}_H>0. Therefore A_H initially increases, and the trajectory enters the interior \{A_H>U_{\min}\}.

In the critical case, \dot{D}_H=0, so \ddot{A}_H=0. The trajectory remains at the equilibrium E^*.

By Theorem 4.3, the forward trajectory converges to (A_H^*,D_H^*). Since A_H^*\ge U_{\min} in both cases, the forward branch remains in \mathcal{K}. \square

---

Appendix V: Proof of Lemma 6.3 (Backward Branch of the Critical Trajectory)

Lemma 6.3. The backward branch \Gamma_- enters the region \{A_H>U_{\min}\}\cap\{D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}}\} and extends to \partial([0,1]^2).

Proof. In backward time, the vector field is (-\dot{A}_H,-\dot{D}_H). At \Gamma(0), -\dot{A}_H=0 and -\dot{D}_H\ge 0. For t<0 small, D_H>D_{\mathrm{crit}} and A_H=U_{\min}. In this region, \dot{A}_H<0, so -\dot{A}_H>0, and the backward trajectory moves to A_H>U_{\min}.

The backward trajectory is bounded (since [0,1]^2 is compact). Its \alpha-limit set \alpha(\Gamma) is nonempty, compact, and invariant. By Lemma 4.2, the boundary \partial([0,1]^2) contains no invariant sets. Therefore \alpha(\Gamma) must be contained in \mathrm{int}([0,1]^2).

But the only equilibrium in \mathrm{int}([0,1]^2) is (A_H^*,D_H^*), which is a forward attractor. In backward time, (A_H^*,D_H^*) is a repeller. Therefore the backward trajectory cannot converge to (A_H^*,D_H^*).

Since \alpha(\Gamma) is nonempty, compact, and invariant, and contains no equilibrium in \mathrm{int}([0,1]^2), it must intersect \partial([0,1]^2). By Lemma 4.2, no subset of \partial([0,1]^2) is invariant. Therefore \alpha(\Gamma)\subseteq\partial([0,1]^2), and the backward trajectory approaches \partial([0,1]^2). \square

