Appendix W: Proof of Lemma E.1 (Properties of the Separatrix)

Lemma E.1. The separatrix S in the critical case has the following properties:

(i) S is a C^1 curve in \mathrm{int}(\mathcal{K})\cup\{E^*\}.

(ii) S approaches E^* tangentially to the slow eigenvector v_s.

(iii) S is invariant under the flow.

(iv) S divides \mathcal{K} into \mathcal{V}_0 and \mathcal{K}\setminus\mathcal{V}_0.

Proof.

(i) The equilibrium E^* is hyperbolic (both eigenvalues of J are negative and distinct). By the stable manifold theorem, the weak stable manifold W^s_{\mathrm{weak}}(E^*) is a C^1 1D manifold. The separatrix S is the branch of W^s_{\mathrm{weak}}(E^*) that lies in \mathcal{K}.

(ii) By definition of the weak stable manifold, trajectories on W^s_{\mathrm{weak}}(E^*) approach E^* tangentially to the slow eigenvector v_s.

(iii) The stable manifold is invariant under the flow by definition: if x\in W^s(E^*), then \phi_t(x)\in W^s(E^*) for all t\in\mathbb{R}.

(iv) Since W^s_{\mathrm{weak}}(E^*) is 1D and passes through E^*, it locally separates a neighborhood of E^* into two components. In \mathcal{K}, one component contains the segment \Sigma_0 (where trajectories remain in \mathcal{K}), and the other contains the exit segment (where trajectories leave \mathcal{K}). Therefore S divides \mathcal{K} into \mathcal{V}_0 and \mathcal{K}\setminus\mathcal{V}_0. \square

