Appendix Z: Final Remarks on Mathematical Rigor

The proofs in this dissertation rely on several standard results from dynamical systems theory and viability theory:

1. Dulac's criterion (Appendix G): A standard method for excluding periodic orbits in planar systems.
2. Poincaré–Bendixson theorem (Appendix G): A fundamental result characterizing the limit sets of planar trajectories.
3. Stable manifold theorem (Appendix E, Lemma E.1): A standard result for hyperbolic equilibria.
4. Viability kernel characterization (Definition 5.1): The viability kernel is the largest forward-invariant subset of the safe set. This is a standard characterization in viability theory.

The proofs are self-contained modulo these standard results. The corrections incorporated in this final version address the errors identified in earlier drafts, particularly the incorrect characterization of the critical case in Theorem 6.4(v).

The central mathematical results—global asymptotic stability (Theorem 4.3), the autonomous viability theorem (Theorem 5.3), and the exact viability kernel characterization (Theorem 6.4)—are established rigorously within the reduced autonomous model. The extension to the controlled case (§7) and the constitutive mapping (§8) are conditional on additional specifications that are left as open problems.

